{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts","id":1029340,"title":"Little Pieces of Heaven - An attempt at rewriting Artifacts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-09T16:49:01-08:00","id":1029340,"title":"Little Pieces of Heaven - An attempt at rewriting Artifacts","content":"So, there's been some discussion on Artifacts lately and it's gotten me wanting to try and formalize my own house rules about them. I've written down my own copy on my Drive but, truth be told, getting it out there is about as necessary as putting pen to paper for me; not only does it require you to explain what you're going for and how you intend to invoke it (and why), it also lets you get feedback on that from people who have no reason not to honest.<br /> <br /> As such, here's my attempts to rejigger Artifacts. Comments and critiques are, as ever, welcomed.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> <b>What This Is:</b> An attempt to rewrite Artifacts, both to make them more distinct from Imbued Items and to give them a role befitting their natures in the stories of the Awakened. Rotes and grimoires are no so potent that it is not hard to see why the Awakened would kill for them. Artifacts, though... Well... I've never honestly felt like they're something a character would kill for. They're interesting, but not murder-worthy.<br /> <br /> This rewrite reshapes the powers of Artifacts significantly, making them more reliable and, hence, more potent. The rewards for possession and study of one are also enhanced in this, beyond the few A-Beats that may fall into a player's lap for their possession. They are intended to be things that would be useful even in the hands of a Mage who is already a master of the arcanum the Artifact draws upon. For these reasons, they are adamantly <i>not</i> intended to be things that player characters collect as trophies. Even a single Artifact being in the permanent possession of a cabal could become a feature in the game's landscape. This will be addressed more later on.<br /> <br /> In addition, a part of this write-up is also to hint at another project coming up; one to differentiate Imbued Items (and other Awakened creations) from true Artifacts, and to try and give each distinct roles in the secret world of the Awakened.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> <b>Artifacts Revisited</b><br /> <br /> What is an Artifact? A lucky Sleepwalker who glimpses such things of wonder may describe it as an object which contains magical power, like a saintly relic that provides miraculous cures. This is an accurate, if mundane, description; each Artifact is indeed an object which may, properly induced, provoke a grand magical change in the world. However, the Awakened know them to be so much more.<br /> <br /> Artifacts are not mere objects imbued with magical power; they <i>are</i> magical power, fallen from the Supernal and crystallized in tangible form. This divinity in mortal shell is described in some Awakened theories as a &quot;fixed idea&quot;; a captured perspective on a Supernal Truth. It is, in essence, a spell in physical form, with its actual shape like an Imago pressed into raw Mana.<br /> <br /> This nature as a fixed idea means that Artifacts are relatively fixed things; producing the same effect every time, without much variation. They do precisely what is in keeping with their natures, and nothing more or less.<br /> <br /> <i>Designing an Artifact</i><br /> <br /> Artifacts are a merit, rated at 3+ dots, depending on how many features or traits they have. Despite their mechanization as merits, it is not recommended that they be allowed to be purchased by players and, instead, are bestowed (and taken away) by the ST during play. The merit dots, instead, act as a useful guideline for the ST on their relative power and value, and for determining their Mana pools.<br /> <br /> Artifacts are designed similarly to Legacy attainments; beginning by determining the most powerful effect and the number of arcanum dots required to achieve it (achieving it in this case including also covering all the Reach required; a Spirit Shielding spell with Instant Casting and Advanced Duration requires 3 dots to achieve). This rating is used to determine the Primary Factor of the spell, as normal, and then additional factors equal to it may be distributed as desired. Once chosen, however, those factors are set, and are the same in every casting (usually; extra triggers may be in place to cast it in a different way, but that's the exception, not the norm).<br /> <br /> If desired, the Artifact's dots may be raised to provide the extra factors for a spell, but it cannot be pushed above five. If an Artifact performs an extended casting, the minimum casting time is one hour, but there is no &quot;usual&quot; time; many are idiosyncratic, such as beginning casting when exposed to the light of sunset and casting at sunrise.<br /> <br /> When activated, an Artifact casts the spell as designed above and achieves success on the casting equal to the number of dots in the arcanum in question that it &quot;possesses&quot;, without any rolling. Artifacts may not be counterspelled but their magical effects may be dispelled. In a Clash of Wills, they do not roll, instead attaining automatic successes equal to the arcanum dots used.<br /> <br /> In addition to this, Artifacts are not activated by an act of will but, rather, by an action, which may be a spoken word or an act. A magical sword bursts into flame when swung; an Artifact key opens portals to any destination when put into a lock and turned. What these triggers are is not immediately evident; many Artifacts have languished in Athenaea for centuries with their secrets still yet to be fully unlocked.<br /> <br /> Artifacts have Mana pools equal to their dot rating. Their dot rating is equal to 3 + the highest arcanum used + 1 per Utility Attainment included + 1 per additional spell included. These pools regenerate at a rate of one Mana per day.<br /> <br /> <i>Example Artifact: The Whispering Lion - Artifact ooooo ooo<br /> <br /> The Whispering Lion is a small lion statue in Egyptian style, not much larger than a house cat, which appears to be carved and polished by hand from natural granite rock. When the statue is set before a dwelling and a ritual is performed involving freshly spilled blood being poured across its teeth, its eyes shine with the light of the full moon. Until the next full moon, evil spirits cannot enter the domicile, nor harm those within it, as the lion`s baleful gaze keeps them at bay. Usually, ritualists do not invoke the lion save at the full moon anyway, as its protection ends at the turn of the full moon, regardless of whether it has been a full lunar month between then and its activation.<br /> <br /> The effects of the Artifact are Space 3`s Ban with the Advanced Duration and Area Reach effects, using Spirit, Death, and Mind to target spirits, ghosts, and goetia. The Ban is keyed to allow in these entities invited by the ones who performed the ritual. Since this requires four dots in the arcanum, the Artifact is considered to have Space 4. Any Clashes of Wills inflicted must contest its four automatic successes.<br /> <br /> In addition, the Lion contains a secondary effect, wherein any ephemeral that tries to breach the Ban is inflicted with an overriding fear, inflicting the Frightened Condition upon them. This is a Ruling effect, combining the Spirit, Death, and Mind arcana. It uses only the Instant Casting Reach effect, meaning it only has two arcanum dots in the effect. The Primary Factor is Potency. Its secondary two factor levels are applied one to Potency and one to Duration. The Lion is considered to &quot;touch&quot; any spirit who tries to enter the area of the Ban.</i><br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> <b>But What About Paradox?</b><br /> <br /> It is said that, when the Awakened reach to grasp the Supernal Realms, their hubris draws in the Abyss. Yet once a spell is in the world, does it cause Paradox? Does it invite in the Abyss? The answers is no. Artifacts, as pieces of the Supernal, are not subject to Paradox in their activation, even in the presence of Sleepers. Despite this, however, the Awakened have ample reason not to use Artifacts in front of Sleepers. For one, the effects of the Artifacts are just as subject to Dissonance and unraveling as any spell.<br /> <br /> More importantly, however, Artifacts <i>themselves</i> are subject to Dissonance. Though a Sleeper does not destroy an Artifact merely by looking at it, recognizing it for what it is triggers the same horrifying response as recognizing an existing spell. Any time a Sleeper witness sees an Artifact unleash its grand powers, it accrues Dissonance; suffering the successes as damage that cannot be repaired by any means short of the blessings of Supernal entities, and such things are rare indeed. In this way, Sleepers essentially permanently diminish the presence of the Supernal through their ignorance; destroying precious fragments of higher reality.<br /> <br /> For this reason, Artifacts are protected violently by the Awakened. While Seers of the Throne have magical sugar daddies throwing diamonds from heaven, even they know they will face the wrath of the Exarchs if they casually allow their priceless gifts to come undone. More than one mystagogue has killed Sleepers just for getting close to where Artifacts are hidden.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> <b>Making It Real</b><br /> <br /> Artifacts are not mundane objects. Each one is a fragment of higher reality given form. That form is representative of whatever Truth they are a part of. In this way, Artifacts take the form of legendary items and precious impossibilities; things that feature prominently in myths and stories that seem impossible in mundane reality. Even the simplest Artifact that gives no signs of being overtly magical still signals that, on some level, it is not mundane. The crystal shines just a bit too brightly; the surface is just a bit too perfect at being what it is.<br /> <br /> All Artifacts appear to be &quot;perfected&quot; versions of whatever form they take, be it as simple as a feather to as grand as a magical chariot. While this does not mean they are all flawlessly beautiful, even an object that represents decay and disease is somehow morbidly fascinating.<br /> <br /> This perfection bestows the following qualities on all Artifacts:<br /> <br /> <i>Imperishable:</i> Artifacts gain Durability equal to the highest arcanum rating they possess.<br /> <br /> <i>Inexhaustible:</i> Even Artifacts that would seem &quot;one use&quot;, like elixirs and potions, are fated never to be forgotten. Such Artifacts either regenerate their apparently lost features or reappear somewhere else.<br /> <br /> <i>Glorious:</i> All Artifacts give off a kind of resonance that can only be described as virtue, in the classic Greek sense. Whatever it is that the Artifact's natural form does, it adds +2 to the equipment bonus. Artifact clothes radiate their natural mood; Artifact knives cut more deeply; Artifact harps cannot help but play elegant music.<br /> <br /> <b>Something To Kill For</b><br /> <br /> In addition to these effects, every Artifact also possesses the following far more important trait:<br /> <br /> <i>Vision of Truth:</i> The physical forms of Artifacts are, in effect, an Imago. By studying this Imago, dissecting it, and coming to understand it, a Mage may gain access to a glimpse of Supernal Truth. By accessing the Deep Information of an Artifact with Mage Sight, a Mage may learn the rote for the spell(s) encoded in the Artifact as if it were a grimoire.<br /> <br /> If a Mage who has learned the rote chooses to cast it, they may use the Artifact as a grimoire. However, the pure, unspoiled Supernal knowledge is more &quot;pure&quot; than that in a grimoire; unburdened by the biases of the author of such Fallen tomes. Reading the book of the Supernal, a Mage may cast such spells with greater haste; allowing them to apply the rote quality when casting with reference even on instant castings (though referencing the grimoire does fill a yantra slot). Understandably, this makes such Artifacts tremendously potent, even for experienced Mages.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Alright, so... That's a lot to get out. I'll leave it at that for now. See what people think.<br /> <br /> So... What do you think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T17:29:02-08:00","id":1029365,"title":"","content":"It definitely accomplishes the goal of making artifacts way more potent and valuable. I'd argue that the corebook made the differences between artifacts and imbued items pretty significant already, this just goes even further. Not sold on the mana regeneration, but there are enough sources of easily obtained mana that it probably doesn't matter much either. Overall, I think I like it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T18:57:55-08:00","id":1029410,"title":"","content":"My goal for Imbued Items - when I get to them - is to hopefully put them in a different narrative space than Artifacts. Imbued Items would, ideally, fall into a role of &quot;all-purpose plot duct tape&quot;; acting as a one-time use tool that lets a character accomplish something they couldn't normally but without ultimately affecting their long-term narrative. If the ST needs a character to have a certain spell that they lack in order to slay a monster, the Imbued Item fulfills the role. They hurl the lightning spear to slay the dragon, after which it is spent; they drink the magic potion. You get the idea.<br /> <br /> If characters want iconic items for their characters or odd magical tools that provide interesting bonuses but aren't much more than a dice bonus, I usually apply those things under &quot;natural magic&quot; (i.e. magical objects that come into existence without the use of Awakened spells) or something that's been enhanced with a relinquished spell. Even small but rather distinctive effects - like a gun that shoots lightning but is, for the most part, narratively no different from a slightly more powerful gun - aren't really worth modeling as one of these. If the player wants a magic gun, just give them a magic gun, not what amounts to a modular talent for magic.<br /> <br /> Glad you like it. The Mana thing isn't really necessary either, so exclude it if you want; it's more a relic of 1e than anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T19:18:04-08:00","id":1029423,"title":"","content":"This is great! I especially like how they're subject to Dissonance now. It gives a really good reason for Mages being so protective of them, even more so than usual.<br /> <br /> I would personally say that Artifacts should be completely immune to any damage that doesn't come from Dissonance (this includes immunity to magic both Supernal and otherwise, unless cast by an Archmage). If they are merely durable, you can break them with Matter, or even mundane equipment. That doesn't sound fitting for such legendary items.<br /> <br /> If an Artifact is destroyed by Dissonance, can a Supernal entity still bring it back? They are Platonic ideals, so I believe it should be possible, even if the Supernal entity must be of high Rank and ask a big favor in return.<br /> <br /> The equipment bonus given by the Glorious aspect feels underwhelming. I'd say it should give bonus Successes equal to the highest Arcanum used + 1 and 8 Again. That puts it on par with the Props of the True Fae.<br /> <br /> Can Artifacts possess a mind of their own? Even if they can, I believe they shouldn't be able to act of their own accord. Instead, they should manipulate people into using them, like many cursed items do. Instead of rolling to manipulate people, they would score automatic Successes equal to the highest Arcanum used.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T20:03:41-08:00","id":1029440,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029423#post1029423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is great! I especially like how they're subject to Dissonance now. It gives a really good reason for Mages being so protective of them, even more so than usual.</div> </div> </div> </div>Glad you like it! And, yeah, my goal for Artifacts is that they should be something a Mage would consider killing to get and killing to keep. Mystagogues especially, really.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029423#post1029423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would personally say that Artifacts should be completely immune to any damage that doesn't come from Dissonance (this includes immunity to magic both Supernal and otherwise, unless cast by an Archmage). If they are merely durable, you can break them with Matter, or even mundane equipment. That doesn't sound fitting for such legendary items.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see why you'd want to but, for me, that feels counterproductive. More often than not, Mages don't want to <i>destroy</i> an Artifact; they want it for themselves. That means the threat of its destruction - either by a jealous rival or their own carelessness - is a persistent factor in the pursuit of them. You need to be cunning and careful or else you might end up tragically destroying the very prize they're after.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029423#post1029423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If an Artifact is destroyed by Dissonance, can a Supernal entity still bring it back? They are Platonic ideals, so I believe it should be possible, even if the Supernal entity must be of high Rank and ask a big favor in return.</div> </div> </div> </div>No reason to say no, but as a general rule, an Artifact represents a piece of that being extruded into the Fallen World. If you were careless enough to muck it up once, odds are good they won't trust you with it again. PCs are always the exception but, generally, most Artifacts are unique and, once gone, they're gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029423#post1029423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The equipment bonus given by the Glorious aspect feels underwhelming. I'd say it should give bonus Successes equal to the highest Arcanum used + 1 and 8 Again. That puts it on par with the Props of the True Fae.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Glorious bit isn't really meant to be that valuable. Making it <i>that</i> powerful would be a spell effect unto itself, not a feature of all Artifacts. Glorious is just meant to represent that an Artifact is a bit of a Platonic Ideal for the shape it takes; it's more of a kind of tactile reminder to the players that the item isn't normal, if you will, than an actual power.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029423#post1029423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can Artifacts possess a mind of their own? Even if they can, I believe they shouldn't be able to act of their own accord. Instead, they should manipulate people into using them, like many cursed items do. Instead of rolling to manipulate people, they would score automatic Successes equal to the highest Arcanum used.</div> </div> </div> </div>If an Artifact does possess a mind of its own - which it very well may - it's a unique feature of that Artifact, not a common rule. That means each will influence people differently. Maybe it has in its nature a certain spell that it casts on those who carry it, say. More commonly, though, Artifacts don't have a consciousness but they do have... Inclinations. Each is a piece of a grander narrative, and that narrative tends to repeat itself around them. A crown that bestows great wisdom that leads to tragedy will see those who wear it often come to the same fate, but whether this is destiny, mind control, sympathy, self-fulfilling prophecy, or simply that human nature tends to guide people down that path when given the relevant power... That is anyone's guess.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Edit: Another reason for Glorious being what it is, is that it prevents Artifacts from instantly being identified as such. If Artifacts were so overwhelmingly potent on first blush, they'd be easy to notice. In practice, Artifacts might go unnoticed as anything more than a &quot;natural magic&quot; trinket; an oddity. At least up until someone tries to unravel its secrets and finds it's actually a piece of divinity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2855,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","user":"Axelgear","date":". 01-09-2017, 11:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T20:54:08-08:00","id":1029455,"title":"","content":"Re: destructibility, you guys can compromise with a bane of some kind. Makes it hard to destroy by hitting it with a stick a lot, but if you know the trick, it's pretty easy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T21:10:32-08:00","id":1029458,"title":"","content":"Rather than make them subject to dissonance I'd make it an Act of Hubris to abuse an artifact, or to let a sleeper witness the effects of one. Striking someone down with a flaming sword from heaven is using the tools of heaven for earthly goals and all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10831-bauran","name":"Bauran","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10831_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T04:03:02-08:00","id":1029541,"title":"","content":"This reminds me of my favorite &quot;I'm gonna play this when rules for it won't suck&quot; concepts: a Mage who ended up in a some kind of accident, and got a lot of stuff (metal, sharp fragments, you get the gist) left inside their body as it wasn't safe to remove it - remove it and the not-yet-mage dies.<br /> The central idea is that this stuff inside turns out to be an Artifact, and removing it *will* kill the Mage - one of Artifacts side effects would be animating the Mage who should be dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","user":"WHW","date":". 01-10-2017, 07:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T04:12:01-08:00","id":1029545,"title":"","content":"If Artifacts were indestructible, you know there would be a Time Mage who would just bomb large areas to dust, check if there is anything left (because if there is, it's an Artifact!!), and reverse time after &quot;conducting research&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T09:53:48-08:00","id":1029640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029455#post1029455\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Re: destructibility, you guys can compromise with a bane of some kind. Makes it hard to destroy by hitting it with a stick a lot, but if you know the trick, it's pretty easy.</div> </div> </div> </div>That doesn't really work, sadly. It makes Artifacts more vulnerable, but in the wrong way. Artifacts are supposed to be vulnerable to the fuck-ups of a Mage; as in all things, when it goes bad, it's the Mage who measured their own noose.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bauran</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1029340-little-pieces-of-heaven-an-attempt-at-rewriting-artifacts?p=1029458#post1029458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather than make them subject to dissonance I'd make it an Act of Hubris to abuse an artifact, or to let a sleeper witness the effects of one. Striking someone down with a flaming sword from heaven is using the tools of heaven for earthly goals and all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those things are already Acts of Hubris, I'd imagine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T14:41:34-08:00","id":1029767,"title":"","content":"Another possibility is just to make outside knowledge that you have an artifact a Death Mark, other Mages WILL kill for it (especially Seers who should have a continuous mandate to hide artifacts away) or something ultimately worse that is drawn by Sleeper reactions, a creature of the Abyss perhaps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T00:03:54-08:00","id":1041134,"title":"","content":"I like your works and I might get a chance to play new Mage character. I dislike writing content for my personal use (like, writing a merit explicitly &quot;for myself&quot;). Could I ask you for crafting me an Artifact?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T05:32:11-08:00","id":1041209,"title":"","content":"I can certainly try. What would you want it to do?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-02T22:56:16-08:00","id":1042825,"title":"","content":"Thanks! Sorry for long wait, but I'm sick lately and don't have strength to post coherently.<br /> I'm enamored with the idea of character that got into an accident and near-death experience, discovering lately that something inside her - leftover metal shards, that artifical heart-augmentation thing, transplanted organ(s), you know the jazz (I know these things aren't very precise, but that's because I'm struggling between picking a Moros or Obrimos) - turned out to be Artifacted as part of her Awakening. I want this thing to be something different than simple &quot;it casts X, Y, Z spells&quot;, modyfing her existence on more deep level, but I don't know how to handle that, especially mechanically (again, I feel uncomfortable writing mechanics for myself to use, and my GM doesn't like to homebrew herself, because she feels her creations are clunky and No Speak Mechanics Language Good No No). I want this thing to be a danger as much as a boon, Supernal parasite or infection that invades your body and Soul irrevocably changing it. Something making the mage more of a monster and victim of the horror at the same time. Something like Raziel's and Soul Reaver's relationship in Legacy of Kain, or Tony Stark's Extremis Virus. Or Cable's technovirus. Yes, I like the &quot;lone hero suffering from a curse that has to be also their source of strength if they want to survive&quot; motif. Wish we had Tremere rules... Yeah, <a href=\"http://marvel.wikia.com/wiki/Techno-Organic_Virus\" target=\"_blank\">http://marvel.wikia.com/wiki/Techno-Organic_Virus</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Techno-organic_virus\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Techno-organic_virus</a> something like this - not in powers, but in relationship to main character and how it changed their life.<br /> <br /> I really like the idea of it being Death attuned somehow (or Abyss attuned, eh-heh-eh, this character is going to be Target For Vivisection from day 1), and offering a way to grow the character on different axis than just Gnosis/Arcana when it comes to her supernatural abilities. I have no idea how to handle it in interesting way, so while asking for a whole Artifact (especially before I decide between two different character concepts that have &quot;accident and being a host to powerful Artifact&quot; as only thing in common) is probably too much, I would kill for at least some guidelines or framework to work with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","user":"WHW","date":". 02-03-2017, 02:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T21:03:20-08:00","id":1043356,"title":"","content":"I like it.<br /> <br /> I dunno how I feel about it being an uber grimoire, though. On the one hand, it's fairly limited, but on the other hand, grimoires are fairly limited themselves. The only advantage over an artifact is that a grimoire would have <i>slightly</i> more spells, making the artifact strictly better in nearly all applications.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T21:37:21-08:00","id":1043375,"title":"","content":"If you really want a change from imbued artifacts i would emphasis on the ability to do things mages can't just do with spells. Something similar to prelacies, which I honestly feel is a waste limiting to seers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T08:27:49-08:00","id":1043567,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar9102_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"9102\" data-vbnamecard=\"9102\">WHW</a> - It sounds like what you're after will need to evolve with the game you're in, but I can at least give it a shot.<br /> <br /> <b>Fragments of Eternity (<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></span>)</b><br /> <br /> Death is the road to awe. When one witnesses the precipice between life and its end, there one may stand at the edge of a great cliff and gaze into the undiscovered country of Stygia. It is often in such moments that Sleepers come face to face with the Lie, and those who struggle through the Mystery Play find their way to Awakening.<br /> <br /> Sometimes, they bring things with them for the journey; things so symbolically bound to them that these items become Artifacts in the process; the physical embodiment of a Supernal thought.<br /> <br /> Fragments of Eternity are Stygian Artifacts created when someone suffers a violent death that sees pieces of the source of their demise thrust into their body and left there. This may be metal or glass shards from a car crash, a broken piece of a blade, or a bullet. These fragments are never something forgettable; they're violently obvious, and anyone bearing such fragments feels them with every move and breath, suffering a constant reminder of their demise.<br /> <br /> (Those with a Fragment of Eternity embedded in them suffer a -1 to all Physical rolls from the pain of a foreign object grinding around in their insides.)<br /> <br /> The first and most obvious feature of a fragment is its connection to death. Whenever a Fragment is within (bearer's Gnosis x 10) yards of a person who has died in the past day or who will die within the next 24 hours, it begins to hurt. How this pain manifests is usually tied to its initial sensation of entry; a bullet burns, glass shards cause a sharp shock, etc. This increases the penalty the object inflicts to -2 as the object tugs at their insides and points them towards the doomed individual.<br /> <br /> (This is a Death/Time 1 Knowing/Unveiling effect.)<br /> <br /> In most cases, this is as far as the story goes. Most Moros prefer to have the foreign object removed than continue to endure its presence. Of those who do not, though, there are... Stories.<br /> <br /> Fragments of Eternity were meant to claim their bearer's life. This sensation is never lost on the bearer; they feel it there, always; a reminder that they <i>should</i> be dead. Some describe feeling the object move, as if it's still trying to kill them; squirming in their guts. They're not wrong.<br /> <br /> If allowed to fester within the Mage, the fragment begins to take root. Individual fragments begin to grow; extruding wires of metal or glass like veins and constituting themselves into a network of threads and nodes. Allowed to continue doing so, they will keep extending through the host's body until they reach vital organs and begin to wrap around them. The fragments slowly infiltrate these tissues and transform them into inhuman structures. Autopsies on Moros who endured the presence of their fragments may find crystalline stomachs, brass leaf intestines, or literal iron lungs. These corpses might be confused for Sariras if not for the fact that they still rot.<br /> <br /> After a period of around six months, the fragments are now permanently a part of the Mage's Pattern; removing them (without sufficiently swift application of regenerative Life magic) is lethal.<br /> <br /> At this stage, the character no longer suffers damage like a living human; their organs are hard and their blood is often replaced with strange substances like motor oil or gasoline. They take only Bashing damage from any attack that does not score an exceptional success and do not risk unconsciousness from having their last health box filled with Bashing. If their final health box is filled with lethal damage, the character appears to die but their soul remains trapped within the matrix of wires within their body. Matter magic to &quot;repair&quot; the character can restore them to whatever mockery of life they now exhibit, so long as it's applied before their body rots away.<br /> <br /> In addition to the above, the character gains 3/3 armour.<br /> <br /> (This is a Death 3 effect.)<br /> <br /> At this stage, though, the character's new bodily additions become hungry. The Artifact, rather than producing its own Mana, <i>drains</i> Mana. This Mana comes from the pool of the Mage who carries it at a rate of one per day. If the Mage cannot pay the due, the Artifact forces Scouring upon them and takes <i>all</i> the Mana from doing so. The character cannot heal the associated damage until they regain at least three Mana.<br /> <br /> During this time, the character feels increasingly morbid and obsessed with death, most often their own. The character is frequently haunted by dreams of an afterlife they have been denied; of an end or rest that is forbidden to them. There is a persistent sensation that they have missed their chance, though for what is hard to say.<br /> <br /> Any time the basic power of the Artifact is triggered, the character gains the Obsessed Condition towards witnessing (or bringing about) the death they know is coming.<br /> <br /> For the few who carry on with such Artifacts, this is often where it ends. Obsessed with their nightmares, many take their own lives or, just as often, are killed by their peers who covet the Artifact within. Many are unwilling to fight back.<br /> <br /> The ones who struggle on despite this, of course, inevitably fail at some point to feed their manavoric organs. When this happens, the character may enter a third and more horrific phase still.<br /> <br /> Hungry for the death it was denied, the Artifact will blossom further. It pushes its way through the bearer's skin; extruding nodules from their arms and hands. These nodules, when commanded, erupt into strange weapons, usually symbolic of how the bearer died - bone spurs for one mauled by animals, glass spears for those impaled on broken glass, bludgeons for a car crash, or even spewing bullets or flames for firearms and explosions. These weapons never count as improvised for the wielder and have a Durability of 4 and Structure of 2.<br /> <br /> In addition to the natural benefit of these weapons, any time they strike, the drain off the dying energies of the victim to feed the Artifact. This causes the weapons to deal (4 - Stamina) extra levels of lethal damage on a hit, with the extra levels dealt transferred to the wielder as Mana. These wounds are <i>never</i> natural in appearance, often putrefying as if inflicted on a long-dead corpse, though these marks tend to fade on healing.<br /> <br /> On top of <i>these</i> benefits, the character's armour rises to 4/4 and they no longer suffer the penalties associated with the Artifact's presence.<br /> <br /> The drawbacks, though, are severe. The greater expenditure has greater costs; the Artifact now drains <i>three</i> Mana a day from the host (and has similarly dire demands if not fed; inflicting three Resistant lethal levels or draining three attribute dots if not fed). Likewise, the host must roll Resolve + Composure whenever below half their maximum Mana to avoid gaining the Obsessed Condition towards feeding.<br /> <br /> (This is a Death 4 effect, mimicking the effects of Devouring the Slain.)<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> There you go. A horrible artifact for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-05T04:51:58-08:00","id":1043921,"title":"","content":"This is extremely amazing and definitely hitting the themes, feel and mood I crave in the game. This is the brand of personal horror I really lacked in our main Mage game; I love the whole grotesque, body horror feel, and I love how you weaved mana sustenance into it. I wonder how you would tackle my Obrimos idea (in this case, &quot;target&quot; being the bioelectric field of your body, including electricity in the brain and neural system; &quot;supernal, Forces-Prime infection of your electric self&quot;) <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.<br /> <br /> I feel that Fragments of Eternity would work best as a set of evolving Conditions, reflecting deeper and deeper stages of the infection. This looks, smells and works perfectly as a Persistent Condition of some sort. When I'm done with being sick (goddamn it), I'll try turning these into Conditions and posting here, if you are OK with hijacking of your thread for that. Actually, I really dig the idea that Paradox poisoning of the Pattern could so similar things, too...<br /> Yes, I really like idea of Magic slowly turning it's users into inhuman monsters, including their physical and spiritual selves, can't help it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}